# Least-privilege deploy identities (rule 235)

**Status:** ACTIVE from DMS-DEPLOY-PRIVSEP-PHASE-2 (2026-11-15). Sprint record:
`docs/sprints/dms-deploy-privsep-phase-2.md`. Extends rule 233 (which keeps application PHP away from root). Production
GO is recorded in the sprint doc, not here.

## 1. The defects it closes

- **OD-6.** The one SSH account used for deploys and checks held `(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL` (a sudoers drop-in on top of
  the group's password rule), and its key sat unencrypted on a workstation: that key was root on production.
- **R1.** `composer install`, `npm ci` and `npm run build` ran as root on every deploy and rollback: every npm
  dependency's lifecycle scripts and every vite plugin ran as root. Even the `composer --version` probe ran from the
  application directory, where composer may activate a plugin installed in `vendor/`.
- **R2.** Five development scripts that create records through model factories were tracked under `storage/`, which
  the runtime user owns: root's checkout wrote them, and anyone could run them against production.
- **R4.** Both superuser restore tools streamed a runtime-owned plain-SQL dump into psql: a dump changed after it was
  written could run psql meta-commands as the PostgreSQL superuser's OS account. The digest the deploy logged was
  computed by the runtime user over its own file, so a resident runtime process could swap the dump before it.

## 2. Invariants

1. **No passwordless unrestricted sudo, for any account.** The declared administrator (`DMS_PRODUCTION_ADMIN_USER`)
   keeps unrestricted sudo **only with its password**: that is the owner-approved break-glass role (rollback,
   restore, BK-2, the trust anchor, provisioning, any host change). Every other account has no sudo at all, except
   the deploy operator.
2. **The deploy operator runs one program.** A dedicated account (its name stays on the host and the workstation,
   never in this repository) whose only authorized key is `restrict,command="<entrypoint>"`; its home, `.ssh` and
   `authorized_keys` are root's, so it can change neither its key nor its options. Its only sudo right is the
   root-owned `DMS_PRODUCTION_OPERATOR_ENTRYPOINT` with argument expressions that admit exactly
   `start --release-id <id> --approved-sha <40-hex>`, `follow`, `status`, `verify <deploy-id>`, `trust-check`,
   `drift-check`, `sudo-policy-check`, `posture` — rendered by `dms_sudo_render_operator_rule` from
   `dms_sudo_operator_expressions`, never hand-written. No `ALL`, no wildcard, no shell or interpreter, no SETENV,
   no tag but NOPASSWD, no Chroot/Cwd, no command-specific Defaults, no `env_keep`/`runchroot`/`runcwd`, `env_reset`
   and a rendered `secure_path` kept, run-as root only (no group). The expressions are sudo regular expressions:
   the host needs sudo 1.9.10 or newer (Biznet: 1.9.15p5); provisioning refuses an older one before any change.
3. **The entrypoint trusts nothing it is given.** It pins its own PATH first, reads no environment variable but
   `SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND`, splits it on single spaces after a strict character check (no shell, glob or expansion),
   validates the words against the same allowlist, and as root re-validates, proves that it, every PATH entry, the
   program, the `scripts/`, `deploy/` and `.git` trees, every tool root runs (git, composer, node and its whole
   installation, npm, php, pg_dump, runuser, systemd-run, sudo, bash, env) are root-owned and not group/other-writable
   (inside those trees a symlink is accepted only when its target is relative and every step of resolving it stays
   inside the same tree, on its filesystem — r3: a stock Node.js install links `bin/npm` into its own `lib/`), clears
   the environment (`env -i`) and runs exactly one in-tree program. Rollback, restore and every
   mutating host action are unreachable from it.
4. **composer and npm never run as root.** Deploy and rollback run them only through `dms_build_run`: as the build
   identity (`DMS_PRODUCTION_BUILD_USER`: system account, no login shell, no sudo, in no runtime or administrative
   group; its home is ROOT's and holds only `cache/`, the identity's 0700 cache, so a build can plant no SSH key,
   forward or user unit; no build process may be running at the preflight) in a transient systemd service with a
   clean environment, the filesystem read-only except the handed trees and `cache/`, the environment files the asset
   build reads (`.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production`, `.env.production.local`) masked by `/dev/null`, `storage/`, `bootstrap/cache` and `.git` inaccessible, loopback denied except the DNS
   stub, private `/tmp` and `/dev`, no setuid, no capabilities, a 30-minute limit, and its cgroup killed when the step
   ends. Root hands `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and `public/build` over only while no build process runs and claims them
   back (`root:root`) before anything loads them, refusing device nodes, FIFOs, sockets, setuid/setgid files,
   a hardlinked file with a name outside the tree (esbuild's own launcher↔binary link stays inside and is allowed),
   foreign entries and any group/other-writable entry; `public/build` holds no symlink. Version
   probes run from `/`, composer with `--no-plugins`.
5. **Runtime trees hold no trusted code.** Only `.gitignore`/`.gitkeep` placeholders are tracked under `storage/` and
   `bootstrap/cache` (rule 233 keeps root's own writes out of them).
6. **A restore needs trusted digest evidence.** `scripts/restore_postgres.sh` and `scripts/restore-rehearsal.sh`
   stream to psql only a root-only staging copy (read once by the dump's owner) whose SHA-256 equals either the one
   `Pre-deploy backup:` line of that deploy's root-only log (`--deploy-id`; record must name this host and that
   deploy; more or fewer lines refuse) or a digest the administrator passes from an independent record
   (`--expected-sha256`: the rollback's own output, an off-server manifest — never a file beside the dump). That
   logged digest is ROOT's: deploy and rollback run pg_dump as root into a private file in the run's root-only
   snapshot directory, hash it there, hand the runtime user the bytes on a read-only stdin
   (`dms_privsep_as_runtime_fn_from`) and refuse a runtime copy that differs. A fresh rehearsal snapshot is hashed
   from root's own copy. Missing, ambiguous or mismatched evidence restores nothing.
7. **Production restore and rollback stay owner decisions.** Neither is reachable by the operator; both need the
   administrator's password (the owner) besides the existing guards (`--force-production` plus the typed
   confirmation; rollback by full SHA, code only).
8. **Fail closed, checked read-only.** `scripts/sudo-policy-check.sh` classifies every account from sudo's own long
   listing and reports `SUDO_POLICY=LEAST_PRIVILEGE` only when all of the above hold, the installed entrypoint is the
   in-tree one (`STALE` is a WATCH: re-provision) and `visudo -c` accepts the policy.

## 3. Changing this area

- A new operator action needs: the entrypoint allowlist, the sudo expressions (`dms_sudo_render_operator_rule`), the
  tests that cross-check both, re-provisioning, and a read-only or release-guarded program. Never add a shell,
  `rollback-vps.sh`, a restore tool or anything that writes outside the release guard.
- Never hand-edit `/etc/sudoers.d`: re-render with `scripts/provision-deploy-identities.sh` (staged name with a dot,
  `visudo -cf`, atomic move, whole-policy `visudo -c`, moved back out on failure).
- A new build tool runs only through `dms_build_run`; the scanner (`build_line_prefix`) rejects any other line that
  runs composer, npm, npx, node, yarn or pnpm. A build step that needs a new writable path names it explicitly.
- Never restore from a digest you read from the runtime-owned tree.

## 4. Operations

- One-time provisioning and cutover: runbook `docs/runbooks/production-host-identity.md` §6f (export of the approved
  release, `--build-identity`, `--operator`, build rehearsal, deploy through the operator, then — only after the owner
  proved the administrator's password with `sudo -k; sudo -v` — `--retire-admin-nopasswd --admin-password-verified`).
  `sudo -v` is the right proof: under the default `verifypw=all` it asks for the password while any of the account's
  rules needs one, whereas `sudo -u root true` would still match the passwordless rule and prove nothing. The
  retirement re-classifies the administrator's listing as it will be without the passwordless rule and refuses unless
  that is `ADMIN_PASSWORD` (a password rule that reaches root; no `!authenticate`, `rootpw`, `targetpw`, `runaspw`).
- Recovery if the operator path breaks: the administrator logs in (`daengtisiams-biznet-prod`) and uses sudo with its
  password; if SSH itself is unavailable, the provider's web console with the same account. Root's password is locked
  on this host, so the administrator's password is the recovery credential: the owner keeps it; it is never typed
  into a chat, a file or a command line by an agent.

## 5. Residual risks (owner-visible)

- The build identity's package caches persist between deploys: a compromised build (malicious package) could poison
  them for later builds. Bounded by the sandbox: it reaches vendor code the runtime runs anyway, never root.
- Composer and npm still resolve dependencies on the host (lockfiles authoritative); CI-built, digest-verified
  artifacts would remove host-side resolution but change the delivery model — a separate owner decision.
- `scripts/rollout-restore-drill.sh` (staging only, refuses production) replays as the PostgreSQL superuser since R6-B6
  (D-Q7). Finding A-1 (2026-11-25, owner directive: remediate, never risk-accept) closed the gap that opened: the drill
  now takes this rule's restore path. It reads the expected digest from the deploy's root-only log (`--deploy-id`) or an
  administrator's independent record (`--expected-sha256`); there is no default backup. It refuses a symlinked,
  foreign-owned or group/other-writable backup and an environment file another identity could write. The owner-role
  preflight, the runtime ownership gate and the replay read one root-only staged copy. The replay runs in psql
  restricted mode under a fresh key, so no backslash meta-command in a dump runs. psql, createdb and dropdb run only
  from a pinned directory (`DMS_PG_BINDIR`, default `/usr/lib/postgresql/16/bin`: canonical, owned by root, not
  writable by others), by absolute path, including inside the shared library. They never run through PATH or the
  version-switching wrapper. psql must answer PostgreSQL 16 at 16.15 or later (owner floor; restricted mode arrived
  with the CVE-2025-8714 fix in 16.10). Otherwise the drill stops before it reads a backup.
  A digest proves only that the bytes are pg_dump's. Before R6-D the runtime owns the schema, and the replay runs its
  planted code as the superuser in several places:
  - COPY evaluates CHECK and domain constraints, generated columns and partition keys;
  - the schema section coerces an aggregate INITCOND or a partition bound through a domain CHECK;
  - SQL/JSON constructors call a type's cast to json;
  - foreign-key validation runs as the session user, through operators and implicit casts.
  A session-local load guard therefore runs before every object pg_dump writes, before every COPY and at the end. It
  refuses such an expression when it calls a user-defined function or operator or a builtin that is not IMMUTABLE (for
  example `query_to_xml`). It also refuses user-defined casts, operators, operator classes and operator families. Two
  independent reviews found these paths, and the drill's tests and the private PostgreSQL 16 harness prove each one
  refused. What remains:
  - the guard is a deny-list. It narrows, but does not prove away, a superuser replay of a runtime-owned schema. The
    structural fixes are a non-superuser restore login that is a member of the owner roles (provisioning, R6-B3/R6-D),
    or a throwaway cluster under an unprivileged OS account. Both are owner decisions. The owner directives of
    2026-11-25 forbid any production restore or restore drill in the meantime;
  - a dump whose trusted digest itself covers malicious top-level SQL (a wrong independent record, or a compromise of
    root before the deploy hashed it) still runs that SQL as the superuser;
  - `scripts/restore_postgres.sh` and `scripts/restore-rehearsal.sh` verify the digest but use neither restricted
    mode nor the load guard. A superuser replay through them can still run schema code the runtime planted, until R6-D
    takes ownership away from the runtime; applying the same stream to them is a separate owner decision;
  - `scripts/restore-rehearsal.sh` still ignores a failed drop of its scratch database (R6-B6-F1, OPEN).
- The CI scanner is a regression guard for reviewed code: it judges each `;`/`&&`/`||`/`|` segment, but deliberate
  obfuscation (`n\pm`, a variable holding the tool name) is out of its reach — review covers that.
- `follow` streams the deploy's output, build output included, to the operator's terminal as it is.
- Account separation is verifiable; human separation is not (CG-07).
